0 @N2863@ NOTE Starting in 1936 the Social Security Administration started to maintain records of each individual who applied for a Social Security Number. The earliest form of these records were known as the “Master Files of the Social Security (SSN
1 CONC ) Holders and SSN Applications”. This was more commonly known simply as the “Enumeration System”. In the 1970s legacy records from this system were migrated and new records were maintained electronically in the Numerical Identification System (NU
1 CONC MIDENT).
1 CONT This collection contains records of individuals with a verified death between 1936 and 2007 or who would have been over 110 years old by December 31, 2007. There are three types of entries in NUMIDENT: applications (SS-5), claims, and death entri
1 CONC es. The records of applications and claims are presented here in this collection. The death entries are available on MyHeritage as the U.S. Social Security Death Index (SSDI).
1 CONT The application (SS-5) records contain information extracted from the SS-5 form “Application for a Social Security Card” or “Application for Social Security Account Number.” Information in the NUMIDENT application entries include applicant’s ful
1 CONC l name, father’s name, mother’s maiden name, sex, race/ethnic description, place of birth, and other information about the application andsubsequent changes to the applicant’s record - such as name changes especially common (and even required) wh
1 CONC en women marry. For about 43 percent of social security numbers, there are multiple application records.
1 CONT The application records preserved by the Social Security Administration do not include records of all social security applications between 1936 and 2007. Information of applications prior to 1973 may be incomplete. There may not be a record for a
1 CONC n individual in both the application records and the death entries and there are nearly 6 million social security numbers in the application records that do not appear in the death entries. And conversely, there are records in the death entries t
1 CONC hat have no corresponding extant record in the application entries.
1 CONT The claim records include information on the type of claim, the claimant’s full name, date of birth, gender, and for about half of the claim records the US state or country of birth. For a small number of social security numbers there are multipl
1 CONC e claim records.
1 CONT Records in this collection may have place names that were abbreviated or personal names that were truncated in the data supplied by the Social Security Administration. MyHeritage has corrected and expanded many of these when possible but other va
1 CONC lues remain abbreviated or truncated.
